#{[{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106673} :activity/id #uuid "e8bbfc2d-b5f4-57c0-9027-1b66b844109b" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106673 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107090 :tutorial/content "Given a matrix $A,$ an **eigenvector** of $A$ is a *non-zero* vector $\\mathbf{v}$ such that \n\n$$\nA \\mathbf{v} = \\lambda \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nfor some scalar $\\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ The scalar $\\lambda$ is called the **eigenvalue** corresponding to the eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nFor example, consider the matrix \n![](images/63/63e2c65a23a0680412a14084d543b6cc19c0529fd80863b09c99987d309eaa4f.svg) \nand the vector ![](images/b7/b7811b74d8d3075357925864b5be27f57b292dadca9c04d222954baa1e66ba2a.svg) Computing the product $A \\cdot \\mathbf{v},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe've discovered that $A {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}} = {\\color{red}-2} {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}.$ Therefore,\n\n- the vector ![](images/13/13a28e543647ede7a88b30fb652bd7fba6c9adb8ee3142161a102de947ef2d2a.svg) is an *eigenvector* of $A,$ and\n\n- the scalar $\\color{red}\\lambda=-2$ is the *eigenvalue* corresponding to the eigenvector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}.$\n\n**Caution:** Remember that an eigenvector must be a *non-zero* vector. This is very important." :tutorial/id #uuid "1014a8e8-38ce-568b-86d7-22f32ef8f697" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3557 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9b7139a7-3f19-5d72-857b-7a01a291e46b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106674}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106674 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105768 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4e9867f-15ba-5073-a20f-542045fced08"} :step/id #uuid "4921a5cc-f26d-5b7a-9865-e447eec1d53e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106746}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106675 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb"} :step/id #uuid "64cdc019-4015-5867-b014-691bcc52330a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106676 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105770 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f5608f-a9f5-5e6a-b308-540ed8b29259"} :step/id #uuid "6e9a9912-8def-5a76-892f-0608e119d89c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106675}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106711 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49c501da-161c-53be-8349-571cc6a07975"} :step/id #uuid "5b97f2fc-3025-5372-9dc4-19c05d084b76" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106676}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106746 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107098 :tutorial/content "Eigenvectors and eigenvalues can be interpreted geometrically.\n\nFirst, recall that if the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is the standard matrix of some linear transformation ![](images/80/80d488fc9d8168cecdf882f1aa178325bb36bd387dd9f86f2b0cd98a5c54f4dc.svg) then the product ![](images/dc/dcc52971e9a7e86ebbe8bf972c562fce13d389f9261b69cba05dcabb1426b9dc.svg) gives the image of ![](images/4f/4fa2300af121b336bb0d7845b8e2b3e34be5a517a1bca45b4ec7b6514b2c544d.svg) under the action of ![](images/a1/a1af41bdb2f2fe410c6f8e02d1a2a0048f0aa470ceef4508753090444ca039be.svg) The image of ![](images/4f/4fa2300af121b336bb0d7845b8e2b3e34be5a517a1bca45b4ec7b6514b2c544d.svg) under the action of ![](images/c1/c15eeb9e7a3ff525fbdcd2f175622bd034f0b34899224f9ce2e08a5300d625de.svg) can appear quite different from the original vector ![](images/92/9221d4dc55c1df8da019956ea23f9d4f9ff3f5b04921ec2158804a31a50e636b.svg)\n\nHowever, for an eigenvector ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) with corresponding eigenvalue ![](images/4a/4a2f3891f0b4c8b852767d556e1607028035219b70ab83b4910e95159a930b9c.svg) we get\n\n![](images/ee/eed93a562368727b490c2b63d14f46580dcd0439ac90df23a23f75a31de9e971.svg)\n\nIn other words, when the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is applied to an eigenvector ![](images/b2/b2766af4a0fe79e81fbd5ac113f0446ce1d111def401abbf09ced1ee62ca1cf1.svg) the image of ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) is a scaled version of the original vector ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg), and the scale factor is ![](images/44/44db815dcaf0a9d405026460a655495e055df7e55a785f542f8920ca90af5418.svg)\n\nTo demonstrate, consider once again the following matrix:\n\n![](images/43/43e76f5a5e889ec18a6893be8cdbf4837d3dedfab149a0981ce6619a62718170.svg)\n\nWe've already seen that ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) has an eigenvector ![](images/fe/fef256ade4f52003da67fac5af68ff4b0d4e7f04ef55e72bb0279bdc8f1e8c3c.svg) with corresponding eigenvalue ![](images/b5/b5cdbd2f286044241daca2565a1ab7f70196ecc2cf6503965d9a820faad9b06e.svg) So we have\n\n![](images/2d/2da94bfdde0e3650ded2622748ead5a4c09cabf610f9d181d48ae158d9bd0986.svg)\n\nPlotting the vector ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) and its image ![](images/ab/abe8027351c1645f4c582440b8b536f48f3ec35d008570f3432f322fa468e861.svg) in the plane, we get the following picture.\n\n![](images/9a/9aca41e0597b0b1698e8ec24da9966fd9b7e5fee3951081d8abdb0cc0caaac7b.png)\n\nIn general, when we apply a matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) to an eigenvector ![](images/b2/b2766af4a0fe79e81fbd5ac113f0446ce1d111def401abbf09ced1ee62ca1cf1.svg) the resulting vector ![](images/be/befecb9d885ee3bbbb7313401ca3671428e4aced68a4bfdb4b2fc9a005ea2b43.svg) always lies on the same line as ![](images/1f/1f3d1a60926323e963d7563529669e5cf8210771c8cbb0379f7a7d90e7c6aeec.svg)\n\nIf ![](images/8c/8c453358dacec49655b560be52a99066e3cab1d34cfbdbdbaa7bd74d868fef82.svg) then the matrix stretches the vector. For ![](images/ea/ea6d742051f82ff2f258e08ed09c3b3dc6d4c7f9bbbe40fea718aec8183dc44f.svg) the matrix compresses the vector. In addition, a negative eigenvalue ![](images/c9/c91cda6703c5767aaafeca807b07e466f160cb0ade84472ade5fa85d71a5713e.svg) means that ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) is flipped in the opposite direction as well as scaled." :tutorial/id #uuid "cdf6ca75-e37b-552a-a106-ee8ac2f36a61" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3963 :tutorial/title "The Geometric Interpretation of Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"} :step/id #uuid "ab53090e-96b8-5dcc-b77f-6ff038689cac" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106711}}] :activity/title "The Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186106002}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186048819 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105768 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105620 :question/id #uuid "61bb0d60-a61e-5485-9df5-308e16dfd1aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-5476" :question/problem "Determine if ![](images/5d/5da5dddce497b4b2b8ffe24924714e960a3c43088351e0a2a6d039000ed78035.svg) is an eigenvector of the matrix ![](images/6a/6a73978c951c4ae02d1455b5881a210c3d415bf1beef2f6f41f694c361708422.svg) If so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A\\mathbf{v}=4 \\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda=4.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4e9867f-15ba-5073-a20f-542045fced08" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106092 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4f8ae2b-49ac-5606-bcee-45828d5bd241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102429} {:answer/id #uuid "a897f649-961d-529c-ba03-719efffa4ddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102430} {:answer/id #uuid "3293733b-954f-59af-b524-6f75c79492da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102431} {:answer/id #uuid "39c5b7f8-a538-586d-968d-2a21015e1923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102432} {:answer/id #uuid "69805456-952b-5195-8840-ab244662bf87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102433}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f7b0679b-40ee-5a68-b734-2d24a8d3ab43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102428}] :question/id #uuid "db60ba2d-17a4-5d18-a111-9e878e9d6c01" :question/math-academy-id "q-18237" :question/problem "Determine if $v = \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 8 \\\\ -1 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$.\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106296 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68ff064d-8214-5290-9517-cc101297a026" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321968} {:answer/id #uuid "6c914331-29ac-587b-a8b5-ba1f3586e517" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321991} {:answer/id #uuid "6f9a7fe5-ecaa-5035-82c3-55d752195128" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322013} {:answer/id #uuid "77474c33-ccf0-5cf7-9cf1-1959d414358c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322082} {:answer/id #uuid "dbdf6fbe-5030-54d6-813b-667bac9ee63e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322082} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3e8e768-7a82-5ee0-a374-c065e55f8bff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323650}] :question/id #uuid "b4dcf6a4-b235-571e-8987-add1af756339" :question/math-academy-id "q-36791" :question/problem "Determine if $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$  is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =5v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=5v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243944 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c869c05-72e1-5db4-8f2a-e2faaa09727d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241576} {:answer/id #uuid "5f10cb15-3c38-5c24-8f13-58a522e65b96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242034} {:answer/id #uuid "66400d78-c2e2-5992-a488-703ab81ccb3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242103} {:answer/id #uuid "917ee91e-bae0-5f76-b9f1-02142ea13eb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242459} {:answer/id #uuid "e969f6b1-87b6-548f-8721-e558f664bf21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241576} :answer-field/id #uuid "288fa7f4-53b7-525d-841e-0a5c7e1e3359" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243511}] :question/id #uuid "1e309174-90b1-5dec-b490-77c152128db8" :question/math-academy-id "q-106744" :question/problem "Determine if $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector, then there must exist a real number $\\lambda $ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ Hence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\lambda \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis leads to the following system of equations for $\\lambda :$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=\\lambda \\\\ -2 &=-\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system has no solution. Therefore, $v$ is not an eigenvector of $A.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244106 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "104cca4d-db95-513c-9d44-cc2a379381c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241320} {:answer/id #uuid "32e31133-c8e7-5315-bbfe-f5efbbf3c95f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241629} {:answer/id #uuid "416a9a71-d3c5-5634-9db9-404f460df51c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241767} {:answer/id #uuid "91233414-7760-54aa-8bf0-0a7bdd82b482" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242454} {:answer/id #uuid "efc1d3cf-a7db-5cc3-8175-ffa8a130d068" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241320} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4a34b55-3f54-539c-93ce-3d5b0c0f1987" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243808}] :question/id #uuid "0282c952-693d-5dc9-bc8b-6f8644d776fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-107819" :question/problem "Determine if $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=4v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323871 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fc0c9c0-6f3c-5bb0-b212-c60dd7771e8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321772} {:answer/id #uuid "82c77c10-76df-5ca0-a780-d169fd058443" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322178} {:answer/id #uuid "c11d1924-d4dd-5c85-b7da-2cc6e56981d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322649} {:answer/id #uuid "d0ed88e6-c4c4-5e4b-9123-a9cf3707e6d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322778} {:answer/id #uuid "d28ba49e-8625-52f2-a9d1-fafde471fc5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322778} :answer-field/id #uuid "026ff203-ca8a-5439-a9b5-21104464e530" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323170}] :question/id #uuid "e70ea9d9-6752-5682-88cf-50a5da4311e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-37002" :question/problem "Determine if $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-5v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=-5v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c80a5dc-9352-583c-8572-73f952c10e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336994} {:answer/id #uuid "82073ba0-1b04-55a2-be91-a1683b077fe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337216} {:answer/id #uuid "9058173f-be54-5869-8f04-66bf7604d6cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337340} {:answer/id #uuid "d4edc88e-2232-5b22-bd0f-d01794760374" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337891} {:answer/id #uuid "eb4ed2fb-1160-5044-943c-5b68d1bb783c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337340} :answer-field/id #uuid "5980d238-d65b-558d-93dd-20b965dc3b18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338430}] :question/id #uuid "5e497c53-3e94-5040-a1eb-3a59ea77070d" :question/math-academy-id "q-70917" :question/problem "Determine if $v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector, then there must exist a real number $\\lambda $ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ Hence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\lambda \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis leads to the following system of equations for $\\lambda :$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2 &=2\\lambda \\\\ 3 &=-\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system has no solution. Therefore, $v$ is not an eigenvector of $A.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345104 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8640d078-095b-551c-ba89-0e2beafc0e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343391} {:answer/id #uuid "864c2940-911c-5747-b606-20c85328e120" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343392} {:answer/id #uuid "ae44770a-202a-5f96-9a10-8d2e6a8bd7a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343769} {:answer/id #uuid "f6752ec8-dc6b-5e9e-97b6-f5f379ce2249" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344407} {:answer/id #uuid "fd1ac9d2-353a-5286-9675-250eb3e9ebbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344461} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e7edc45-7bde-50fe-8431-ca2d892025ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344524}] :question/id #uuid "2d3a87ac-015d-5ac1-b497-a6ce1ac3caad" :question/math-academy-id "q-75586" :question/problem "Determine if $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=-3v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345105 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b0b3f27-bb94-59e6-9a1e-43ddbc9929b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343604} {:answer/id #uuid "a8febc30-1322-5173-abb8-2873a57c552f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343725} {:answer/id #uuid "c83df0e6-e982-53f1-974e-5a4512601afd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343997} {:answer/id #uuid "cf2d7adf-79f6-57fc-b3d5-c61fd49b6c12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344061} {:answer/id #uuid "d92d7643-23df-52ce-9e99-2e9864b8018f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343997} :answer-field/id #uuid "da026b09-8f05-574e-98f7-54c03fc90595" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344868}] :question/id #uuid "ad6b2adc-bc53-5c79-b5cd-55ba86c3dfdd" :question/math-academy-id "q-75589" :question/problem "Determine if $v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector, then there must exist a real number $\\lambda $ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ Hence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}=\\lambda \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis leads to the following system of equations for $\\lambda :$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}11 &=3\\lambda \\\\ 13 &=-2\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system has no solution. Therefore, $v$ is not an eigenvector of $A.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399538 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17797bd9-5452-599e-90e2-f82db113737c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399540} {:answer/id #uuid "d97a9b83-e803-56f7-9eb3-247224ecca7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399541} {:answer/id #uuid "9d24ac33-bad1-5eef-bbbc-5c38f32cda9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399542} {:answer/id #uuid "dc542406-6a78-5728-b72b-ed2d72b9ad25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399543} {:answer/id #uuid "b6650df1-dfaf-53a5-afd8-f7fca95b0e9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399541} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd4725f6-2b66-51a3-bec8-dcfb0783e6bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399539}] :question/id #uuid "56a4f3a9-ccca-53d2-8026-09ec5330fdbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-106775" :question/problem "Determine if $v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector, then there must exist a real number $\\lambda $ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ Hence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}=\\lambda \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis leads to the following system of equations for $\\lambda :$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}16 &=2\\lambda \\\\ -8 &=-4\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system has no solution. Therefore, $v$ is not an eigenvector of $A.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411285 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15a7b8b4-79e0-5a5e-b815-4fb73d16b52a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411273} {:answer/id #uuid "789dcf2a-206e-57f2-a9a3-9f05e8688d99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411278} {:answer/id #uuid "9f3222d4-dee2-5a55-8149-170c56576786" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411279} {:answer/id #uuid "b6451324-810e-5585-b20a-6b631b55ec25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411281} {:answer/id #uuid "eb0c0a14-6cf1-5cc9-93cf-929bd01a89bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411278} :answer-field/id #uuid "7db53a96-c2b8-5320-933c-98ee29c6736b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411283}] :question/id #uuid "075cd559-2719-533a-90f7-057ee06afb2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106776" :question/problem "Determine if $v=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector, then there must exist a real number $\\lambda $ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ Hence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}=\\lambda \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis leads to the following system of equations for $\\lambda :$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-10 &=5\\lambda \\\\ -8 &=2\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system has no solution. Therefore, $v$ is not an eigenvector of $A.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalue that Corresponds to a Given Eigenvector"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105769 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105621 :question/id #uuid "f4ff15d3-1a6e-56c0-a7c4-13f18ef1a8e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5477" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ![](images/62/625c1f7a98060333fba9bf4e4172787a6860b796e6de90a12ac2ae4e21611275.svg) is an eigenvector of ![](images/34/34580c90a404ad28b7fb44eb2eebd0465e6cbb2a8bbd339d1981a7dfc8f84a29.svg) that corresponds to the eigenvalue $2.$\n\n- ![](images/20/2034a9bdc4a5f26258a8d72ff9496a72f98c9a9f755502318f2fd6ac0abc6553.svg) is an eigenvector of ![](images/60/60c921f21c6fc641a354cc91d398540c27f0e757bb59bc924f4f7ccaff11a731.svg) that corresponds to the eigenvalue $0.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 5,$ then $2\\mathbf{v}$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda = 5.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A\\mathbf{v}=2 \\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. An eigenvector must be a *non-zero* vector.\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(2v)=2Av=2(5v)=5(2v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(2\\mathbf{v})=5( 2 \\mathbf{v} ),$ and $2 \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0},$ this implies that $2 \\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda = 5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I and III only.\"\n\n**Note:** Statement III is true in the general case, too. In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106297 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fa458c3-7282-53ea-a5bc-e0d9c9649ae4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477265} {:answer/id #uuid "6323e003-b566-54fd-8ad4-a54a9bcf1cc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477265} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ff5f153-df81-5564-84ec-df4c0c2a6cb4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477602} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "927430a2-7643-57f4-820b-091a125a9885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477267} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb94d42-b075-53e5-a318-c0b8c764abd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477268} :answer-field/id #uuid "783247a3-0ebe-54a6-98ea-9862b85cfe12" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477603} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "514a35aa-74e7-586d-b428-f5e95b6fdef4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477269} {:answer/id #uuid "aec979f1-be2c-5a54-b2f4-f82b778d6eaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477269} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8624b7d-1161-5bdc-b93a-f2003b597423" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477604}] :question/id #uuid "2b717856-6a11-5857-bbda-def4fc991b4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-36792" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$\n- **II.** $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0.$\n- **III.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =6.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1$ (but not $2$).\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{v}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=0v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(3v)=3Av=3(2v)=2(3v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(3v)=2(3v),$ and $3v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2$ (but not $6$).\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106298 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fff2517f-6ee9-5857-849b-e8bac0566892" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459883} {:answer/id #uuid "5804ba63-871c-5ec3-9143-f1db8543491f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459884} :answer-field/id #uuid "556c67f5-271f-5f49-84eb-27b172350fdc" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460808} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02048e2e-d9e1-557d-8b5d-99f7bdac06ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459885} {:answer/id #uuid "9ee4cd8c-6049-5a47-91b4-7b276dbb6b7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459886} :answer-field/id #uuid "554a250b-6b90-5daf-85cd-bd88cf2dcbb0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460809} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "319ac9f3-074e-58d8-9580-d87719d9124d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459887} {:answer/id #uuid "ec56c825-4131-57f6-85e5-525e8c9cfb15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459887} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae7a14e2-54a4-5d30-9573-5c686b8bbc8c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460810}] :question/id #uuid "c27f53bd-6979-5472-9b95-f9c6b5cc1260" :question/math-academy-id "q-37087" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1.$\n- **II.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ then $3v$ is also an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda =-7.$\n- **III.** $v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $0.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(3v)=3Av=3(-7v)=-7(3v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(3v)=-7(3v),$ and $3v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-7.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\n- Statement III is false. An eigenvector must be a *non-zero* vector.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244030 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d58913e0-0a2e-503b-895a-9e8a62183598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447884} {:answer/id #uuid "431ce8d2-3792-5889-8d11-0ad0193ca84e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447885} :answer-field/id #uuid "f788e45d-0eb5-52b3-a555-a20ba4a7e688" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448982} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "246df31b-44fb-5288-810d-e408e57f7633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447886} {:answer/id #uuid "9d8cf59f-c8d2-5114-8a53-9044ab7758d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447886} :answer-field/id #uuid "485057f3-721a-5540-bdfe-9c71b7bee298" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448983} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7efe3c0-c40d-5ad1-93ce-b169cedbc81c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447888} {:answer/id #uuid "89361e74-53be-59dc-b7e9-2d650cec0404" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447889} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3e25578-3be1-5e27-a25b-8070d738ce2d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448984}] :question/id #uuid "eace6f7a-724d-598c-b716-132ee8202185" :question/math-academy-id "q-107137" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{5}.$\n- **II.** An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =6$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the same eigenvalue $\\lambda =6.$\n- **III.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then $7v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =2.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement is true. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=5v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=5{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $5$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=\\frac{1}{5}v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =6$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{6}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(7v)=7Av=7(2v)=2(7v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(7v)=2(7v),$ and $7v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $7v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244045 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa87efff-4a12-56eb-a8fd-30204b0128fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447914} {:answer/id #uuid "4674332e-4632-5d4c-b6e9-889347caedd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447915} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee73d125-3aa6-5c82-880d-64be3daa5022" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448997} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d240ce2-a819-5de6-9ea8-364883e337ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447916} {:answer/id #uuid "fdeef0e6-f504-51f6-b05a-04420b84e548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447916} :answer-field/id #uuid "658234fd-f35d-56ed-9746-688865f1db64" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448998} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a78808fb-0db9-59dc-916a-c7ff9581c064" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447918} {:answer/id #uuid "57744455-0207-5c3b-80ce-66fb801d7ec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447919} :answer-field/id #uuid "4063c812-d499-5f4b-adec-12943b0570ef" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448999}] :question/id #uuid "580e535d-24ca-5116-ba35-64ccc5c2ef28" :question/math-academy-id "q-107314" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{7}$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =7.$\n- **II.** An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =1.$\n- **III.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2},$ then $-v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=\\frac{1}{7}v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{7}{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $7$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=7v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $7.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{-1}=-1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(-v)=-Av=-(\\frac{1}{2}v)=\\frac{1}{2}(-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(-v)=\\frac{1}{2}(-v),$ and $-v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $-v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244115 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccffe726-ee51-5e38-82f6-e00cb9bc6c33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447950} {:answer/id #uuid "10830c4b-8858-530c-855b-cb0eb6404dbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447950} :answer-field/id #uuid "082071d0-4be9-5965-b725-b0852043bd08" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449015} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c189d83a-2ee8-541b-acb8-4506740a7835" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447952} {:answer/id #uuid "79efbd22-8415-5e36-8323-929f43ac9b5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447953} :answer-field/id #uuid "05f42b2e-8f6e-5810-a2ac-aac6067c0c01" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449016} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75a10b66-5a8f-5871-9339-219b0e3e74d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447954} {:answer/id #uuid "f07c08ea-4ded-5969-9a0d-3e0fe297bfa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447954} :answer-field/id #uuid "cefce898-b060-52ac-83be-8cd47e2e1931" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449017}] :question/id #uuid "5fa81e71-e4f5-5405-bfdd-41cd7387b276" :question/math-academy-id "q-108044" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$\n\n- **II.** $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3.$\n\n- **III.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2,$ then $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-6.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1$ (but not $-1$).\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=-3v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(3v)=3Av=3(-2v)=-2(3v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(3v)=-2(3v),$ and $3v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (but not $-6$).\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323843 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ed2777a-34cb-534d-a3c0-f46d98a7f57f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467350} {:answer/id #uuid "382ea96b-f840-5089-97ad-613a3c3ea86b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467351} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf044f59-5458-5a3f-9122-e042b2ce2609" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468396} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56892055-3446-54d3-b7c7-091f7041424b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467352} {:answer/id #uuid "97140039-2706-5181-86d8-83d99a14c98f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467352} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b33e161-c9a5-5c82-a2b4-80ffc8368590" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468397} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c95eee0-8ca5-5722-b7c5-52773baa10a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467354} {:answer/id #uuid "af46bc38-7ce0-5c3a-9720-7658d86d8093" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467355} :answer-field/id #uuid "d185ed44-db67-5969-a945-aa27c7caa20f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468398}] :question/id #uuid "187c7a1d-e687-5fd7-b36a-8bd4869c300f" :question/math-academy-id "q-36678" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $2.$\n- **II.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then $-v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-2.$\n- **III.** $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $0.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=2v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(-v)=-Av=-(2v)=2(-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(-v)=2(-v),$ and $-v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $-v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2$ (but not $-2$).\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\n- Statement III is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=0v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323858 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83ea7e4d-2142-5b09-923c-d1732b846aef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467356} {:answer/id #uuid "d55181ee-827a-5082-8727-316c1c665376" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467357} :answer-field/id #uuid "80fb52f1-d337-52da-ab2b-f0e26b72af8a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468399} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69f8eeec-acb8-50ef-b931-56646b1a48a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467358} {:answer/id #uuid "95027d50-dda7-59a1-90d9-fcb2fc8d7016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467358} :answer-field/id #uuid "f91083d4-1dec-5899-9c25-5a1767f35d18" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468400} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8cdfafa-f8c6-57ed-9615-9c048196643d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467360} {:answer/id #uuid "773e58d3-1733-5646-800f-a4d36ca8f057" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467361} :answer-field/id #uuid "c66eca36-f5d2-521d-aeda-fb7f80625ed9" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468401}] :question/id #uuid "1d99b1dd-fcce-5575-b1d6-40adbcb0e805" :question/math-academy-id "q-36865" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{3}.$\n- **II.** An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the same eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$\n- **III.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3,$ then $5v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-3.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=3v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=3{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $3$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=\\frac{1}{3}v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(5v)=5Av=5(-3v)=-3(5v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(5v)=-3(5v),$ and $5v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $5v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323877 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33f5d441-8d4b-58c1-8c22-addd3278df62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467362} {:answer/id #uuid "5290a625-f39a-53d8-892c-452c72d705f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467363} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7fc9d30-3ccb-5565-83ba-123a94e4a7f6" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468402} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "412514e3-a9e7-5613-a307-779d4d58279b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467364} {:answer/id #uuid "ee9e4674-ca29-59e5-8966-974fa679ea90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467365} :answer-field/id #uuid "32d88f51-d3f8-5b52-a864-157037e8ae37" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468403} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4c67b74-bb04-5491-ad72-7bd19c8d5879" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467366} {:answer/id #uuid "3b128b1a-4960-589c-b9b3-e50d9b123f91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467367} :answer-field/id #uuid "102ab22e-5d62-5644-8b69-6d1e5e3127ad" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468404}] :question/id #uuid "5bcdf208-8bdf-5d43-b755-321074d52b2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-37090" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $5$ then $10v$ is also an eigenvector corresponding to $5.$\n- **II.** $v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $6.$\n- **III.** $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $4.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(10v)=10Av=10(5v)=5(10v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(10v)=5(10v),$ and $10v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $10v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =6\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =6v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=6v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =6.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=4v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345121 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fba09f7-6fb0-5d6c-8424-e721e8481780" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468166} {:answer/id #uuid "1cfcaae9-18c3-556e-876b-dbe83dd13637" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468166} :answer-field/id #uuid "1111e0c2-88ed-5b55-afd9-e459c3ebe34f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468804} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3eda578-a166-50a0-a055-3491f285c249" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468168} {:answer/id #uuid "162f9645-e89a-5701-ad26-d9ed38cd8047" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468169} :answer-field/id #uuid "c285d321-3810-5e14-84d2-43bf04bf70f6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468805} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b017c3c-7510-5e3d-b988-1b2e3d35ce99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468170} {:answer/id #uuid "178b845d-847d-59e6-9f16-2bed3653076e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468170} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a6cf479-04ac-5307-9b8b-3196833e508d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468806}] :question/id #uuid "7a5c031b-68ff-5d30-80f0-79dec42f9067" :question/math-academy-id "q-75729" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =-2.$\n- **II.** An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the same eigenvalue $\\lambda =1.$\n- **III.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then $7v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement is false. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=2v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=2{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $2$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=\\frac{1}{2}v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{1}=1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(7v)=7Av=7(2v)=2(7v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(7v)=2(7v),$ and $7v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $7v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345122 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "caa80f00-8548-5f74-b6fb-5dbccba58f42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451645} {:answer/id #uuid "b3040f07-0197-5026-ab47-91dd85bce65c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451646} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d2b06d5-adbe-536a-a0fa-c5c41b302478" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452670} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3228962-8156-5d0f-97a3-009bbc637e90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451647} {:answer/id #uuid "07258935-c7b9-559d-82e2-b673b9b5d690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451648} :answer-field/id #uuid "4854e4f1-f53c-547d-b393-efd9f0eccb7e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452671} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e09fd43d-de8c-5999-95be-1fd16231599c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451649} {:answer/id #uuid "b4e0a7e6-a439-5558-9140-6a92f277aa45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451650} :answer-field/id #uuid "931bc191-690d-5796-9a1c-a924d4fb2c87" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452672}] :question/id #uuid "bb537936-d19a-5b11-8425-d86d338ccdb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-75730" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =4.$\n\n- **II.** An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the same eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$\n\n- **III.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5,$ then $-v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =5.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=\\frac{1}{4}v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{4}{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $4$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=4v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $4.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{-1}=-1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(-v)=-Av=-(5v)=5(-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(-v)=5(-v),$ and $-v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $-v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411286 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee9114a1-aa7f-5df3-814e-651d0530d5ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473326} {:answer/id #uuid "032cba6f-622b-5534-9122-a6d2e0064c5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473326} :answer-field/id #uuid "86c73323-d68f-52e7-bb08-f5a7c5c876ad" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474058} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20ef3ca8-ce71-5b0e-9160-127af09d7f2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473328} {:answer/id #uuid "2b7e1916-223f-548d-9df4-0fcfee646ccd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473328} :answer-field/id #uuid "55a5184c-a43a-5418-856c-b2919b5f0f7f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474059} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b23bec9-7f3a-5e08-adee-f648ba08bc5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473330} {:answer/id #uuid "c58c188c-bab8-504b-94fd-1ab2f97a5ab8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473331} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c1a60f6-a246-55e5-8725-fa3150b13681" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474060}] :question/id #uuid "0ddf6872-c9fc-59bb-a8a4-f3c287d11e0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75724" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1.$\n- **II.** $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5.$\n- **III.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3,$ then $2v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-3.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-1v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (but not $1$).\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{v}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-4{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=-4v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-4$ (but not $5$).\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(2v)=2Av=2(-3v)=-3(2v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(2v)=-3(2v),$ and $2v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $2v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105770 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105622 :question/id #uuid "6cba45d6-5a69-5b83-937c-c19368aaeea9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5478" :question/problem "An eigenvector of ![](images/3b/3b3b1d2eb2faaa02d5c758f820aa597ad2276365906ca16d701a42a0cdcda5af.svg) corresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda=6c$ is  ![](images/7c/7c5842e4b605873d4d965b3d5d8a504ba8bb8610ed8e78d9df0dd186232f9e3b.svg) Determine the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2c\\end{bmatrix} & =6c\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2c\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2+2c \\\\ 1+4c\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6c \\\\ 12{c}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2+2c &=6c \\\\ 1+4c &=12{c}^{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2+2c=6c \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad c = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $c=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+4c & =12{c}^{2} \\\\ 1+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} & =12{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\ 3 & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solutions of the system is $c=\\dfrac{1}{2}$."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f5608f-a9f5-5e6a-b308-540ed8b29259" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106091 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fddca25f-3c70-550f-bd4a-80579e521018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102423} {:answer/id #uuid "06fd8064-2233-58ad-b2f9-5845b6742393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102424} {:answer/id #uuid "447eead5-d935-5bb2-a0a2-ce4c434223a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102425} {:answer/id #uuid "4d8af52e-f4c3-5825-9bfe-62f0e39925d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102426} {:answer/id #uuid "7e0c0f8f-258b-578f-9094-081d5793b719" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102427}] :answer-field/id #uuid "38888f85-379e-5d7a-ab80-72bb4dc075e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102422}] :question/id #uuid "6b393078-abae-5efa-aec2-dc2746104768" :question/math-academy-id "q-18235" :question/problem "An eigenvector of $A = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ corresponding to an eigenvalue $λ = 2$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2c\\end{bmatrix}$. Determine the value of $c$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106093 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abeee3e9-d606-5a26-abe3-3e38789cfa0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102435} {:answer/id #uuid "b25ee212-7d60-5991-afe0-21f6305f7716" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102436} {:answer/id #uuid "3c0d5d65-316f-53ed-8094-0d9c4782c801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102437} {:answer/id #uuid "3e79f34e-7ad4-5e27-afbd-72d13ea1c8b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102438} {:answer/id #uuid "720b94ab-c076-543d-bc8e-8c788348c6b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102439}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b1dbb92b-4ed1-5faa-b47d-950b85a7a8b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102434}] :question/id #uuid "cddf9a9d-717f-56d1-8ffb-bec00b127a39" :question/math-academy-id "q-18238" :question/problem "An eigenvector of\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\ncorresponding to an eigenvalue $λ = 2k + 1$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3k - 2\\end{bmatrix}$. Determine the value of $k$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243959 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d2f9945-a5f6-5def-9d77-fc8387e61030" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241294} {:answer/id #uuid "252c1bee-cb8b-58e2-9ac5-494a6d6e7db4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241500} {:answer/id #uuid "291b579c-d866-5be8-bf43-b0157fef8a8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241540} {:answer/id #uuid "501c0760-6d85-537d-98d6-dc8aefa92ec2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241893} {:answer/id #uuid "b922c98a-fe81-57dc-a76c-c813de524b0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241893} :answer-field/id #uuid "51a38513-1030-57e2-8851-588b150f9592" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243596}] :question/id #uuid "3de44d15-e3e4-5979-86e6-277d8cce23b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-106828" :question/problem "The eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ -k & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =k-1$ is  $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Determine the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ -k & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} & =(k-1)\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-16 \\\\ -4k+4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4k-4 \\\\ -4k+4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-16 &=4k-4, \\\\ -4k+4 &=-4k+4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-12=4k\\,⟹\\,k=-3.\n$$\n\nThe second equation is true for any value of $k.$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243978 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "858437e3-3652-5f5a-a9fb-ca8fc7390f7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242348} {:answer/id #uuid "b8d1800e-9609-5607-8be3-6c382ee50df1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242801} {:answer/id #uuid "c7107059-827c-560b-8d9f-5dec723cbf18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242940} {:answer/id #uuid "ddabf741-8740-5dce-a65b-f445582a5f70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243152} {:answer/id #uuid "e014fd05-4a00-5b4b-95c6-09101e5ca501" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243152} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f19f246-d052-5248-9862-c5ee406c8732" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243526}] :question/id #uuid "327cb7ee-35f9-5f98-b530-dbab7d3417be" :question/math-academy-id "q-106902" :question/problem "The eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}k & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2k\\end{bmatrix}$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =k+2$ is  $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ k-1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Determine the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}k & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ k-1\\end{bmatrix} & =(k+2)\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ k-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2k+1 \\\\ -2+2{k}^{2}-2k\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-k-2 \\\\ {k}^{2}+k-2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2k+1 &=-k-2, \\\\ -2+2{k}^{2}-2k &={k}^{2}+k-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-k=-3\\,⟹\\,k=3.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $k=3$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2+2{k}^{2}-2k & ={k}^{2}+k-2 \\\\ -2+2\\cdot (3)^{2}-2\\cdot 3 & =(3)^{2}+3-2 \\\\ 10 & =10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ce2267d-c0cc-5e5a-848f-0100eaecef60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241581} {:answer/id #uuid "3607fff1-e41e-511d-b071-e4c1a09445a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241654} {:answer/id #uuid "6106f89f-312b-5a37-a6b7-93570e0ec2ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242052} {:answer/id #uuid "8ccfd407-51a3-5eac-bf17-0ed15023e32a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242423} {:answer/id #uuid "eb32c52e-c658-5dca-8ed1-11728eb9e846" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241581} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf57e767-130c-5f77-89c7-75482d91ac1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243801}] :question/id #uuid "76913ab7-7467-5c71-9708-e8c16db072c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-106945" :question/problem "An eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ corresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda =6$ is  $\\begin{bmatrix}c+32 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}.$ Determine the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}c+32 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} & =6\\begin{bmatrix}c+32 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4c+236 \\\\ 2c+28\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6c+192 \\\\ 72\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4c+236 &=6c+192 \\\\ 2c+28 &=72\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n2c+28=72\\,⟹\\,c=22.\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n4c+236=6c+192\\,⟹\\,c=22.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $c=22.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243987 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11bca0a2-614b-50fd-87dc-eb967675e2fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241333} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6f56e4-9815-5dda-9f18-707a058e88f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242038} {:answer/id #uuid "c5428e71-fdc1-5292-bb47-29f43f29145c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242919} {:answer/id #uuid "fd33897e-31a0-5885-b899-87b6d4853a13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243412} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3a2465-d2fa-5709-98fd-7d830e35ecb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241333} :answer-field/id #uuid "05e9ea84-8e38-5ba9-ae83-afa20ad22f9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243448}] :question/id #uuid "dcb8db9c-1fc6-56a1-9d1f-daa26c43ddf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-106946" :question/problem "An eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ is  $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2c\\end{bmatrix}.$ Determine the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2c\\end{bmatrix} & =3\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2c\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1-4c \\\\ -2+8c\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6c\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1-4c &=-3 \\\\ -2+8c &=6c\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n1-4c=-3\\,⟹\\,c=1.\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-2+8c=6c\\,⟹\\,c=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $c=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244107 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1acbfa7e-1097-5f7d-83e6-c2b9ac3e982f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241410} {:answer/id #uuid "2c6761a4-5b41-5bee-afea-b8bc7e3ff850" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241574} {:answer/id #uuid "357cbcbd-820d-57b0-98af-1f34bcfbd25f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241647} {:answer/id #uuid "8bd21a75-41a6-5ee5-a109-306cccd713be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242419} {:answer/id #uuid "9f9da265-adb2-52ac-a03f-18c2bc64d1e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241574} :answer-field/id #uuid "320e84bc-9f0a-5f6c-a0c9-8582a9a6e1f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243531}] :question/id #uuid "e548fde0-64fe-56ce-a880-74d36f5eace9" :question/math-academy-id "q-107837" :question/problem "An eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 25\\end{bmatrix}$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7$ is  $v=\\begin{bmatrix}c+1 \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}.$ Determine the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}11 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 25\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}c+1 \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix} & =7\\begin{bmatrix}c+1 \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}5c+11 \\\\ 13c-12\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}7c+7 \\\\ 7c\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5c+11 &=7c+7 \\\\ 13c-12 &=7c\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n5c+11=7c+7\\,⟹\\,c=2.\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n13c-12=7c\\,⟹\\,c=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $c=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244108 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "273e62f3-6609-56a7-9f9c-2f828cee49de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241518} {:answer/id #uuid "635e199a-7df1-555e-ad95-cd8ebeff5844" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242075} {:answer/id #uuid "9e018857-7a03-58b2-a2e8-2b369d545876" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242563} {:answer/id #uuid "bd0396fb-d3c0-599c-b302-05a3a09b022b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242840} {:answer/id #uuid "fa286430-0097-5d28-a925-5681e6764598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242840} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0a27bf4-3dfa-5e87-8e01-d2eefbd768d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243757}] :question/id #uuid "242d3574-f624-5b15-a035-897cf33da567" :question/math-academy-id "q-107842" :question/problem "The eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -8+k \\\\ 3 & 5k\\end{bmatrix}$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2k$ is  $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Determine the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -8+k \\\\ 3 & 5k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =2k\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}k-10 \\\\ -6+5k\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4k \\\\ 2k\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}k-10 &=-4k, \\\\ -6+5k &=2k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nk-10=-4k\\,⟹\\,k=2.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $k=2$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6+5k & =2k \\\\ -6+5(2) & =2(2) \\\\ 4 & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287815 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "000e3561-1544-5dc9-8c11-17c48b422a41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284787} {:answer/id #uuid "097c3642-1c4d-5f84-8e55-34a351e7fb55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284868} {:answer/id #uuid "0cd337c9-0e8e-5c98-801f-ee9718e01ded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284893} {:answer/id #uuid "201af8ed-db42-5e03-815b-dbf694b92e78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285075} {:answer/id #uuid "fad2ed4b-35b0-525e-8240-933b2bc19e1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285075} :answer-field/id #uuid "a631f2f2-9eef-524a-8c00-16323ea7e0c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287482}] :question/id #uuid "ac23d8ea-7894-5e1d-9e2b-923ff426fb50" :question/math-academy-id "q-18233" :question/problem "An eigenvector of $D=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 10 \\\\ -7 & 11\\end{bmatrix}$ corresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda =6$ is  $\\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix}.$ Determine the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $D$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Dv=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Dv & =\\lambda v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 10 \\\\ -7 & 11\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} & =6\\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-8c+140 \\\\ -7c+154\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6c \\\\ 84\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-8c+140 &=6c \\\\ -7c+154 &=84\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-8c+140=6c\\,⟹\\,c=10.\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-7c+154=84\\,⟹\\,c=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $c=10.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345108 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "163f86d2-fdb8-5f21-84f8-737fdc9a2edf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342433} {:answer/id #uuid "504eb653-acec-5ae9-a3f1-4c61020829f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342904} {:answer/id #uuid "64b87220-b079-5d53-b2c3-64bdc5bb99a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343092} {:answer/id #uuid "a94a82b6-33ed-590a-969a-6c33c2ea9547" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343728} {:answer/id #uuid "dabe1242-62e5-5bc0-8259-75a8a4c362bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343728} :answer-field/id #uuid "43e5d8ce-5e7d-508c-8cea-c2c668a22fc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344603}] :question/id #uuid "e32f6312-bf7a-5fb4-bf70-bbf6dad31c7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-75610" :question/problem "An eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$ \ncorresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda =5k-4$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3k-1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Determine the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3k-1\\end{bmatrix} & =(5k-4)\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3k-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2+2(3k-1) \\\\ -10+8(3k-1)\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2(5k-4) \\\\ (5k-4)(3k-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}6k \\\\ 24k-18\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}10k-8 \\\\ 15{k}^{2}-17k+4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6k &=10k-8 \\\\ 24k-18 &=15{k}^{2}-17k+4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n6k=10k-8\\,⟹\\,k=2.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $k=2$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24k-18 & =15{k}^{2}-17k+4 \\\\ 24\\cdot 2-18 & =15\\cdot {2}^{2}-17\\cdot 2+4 \\\\ 30 & =30,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345109 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0965e63c-f7d0-50df-a452-4c16065920da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342316} {:answer/id #uuid "1efcc603-454f-501c-a026-723a25cfe22b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342495} {:answer/id #uuid "4c06d9d3-2107-5bc8-a214-ea9004c21c28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342865} {:answer/id #uuid "7155f520-f98e-5dc9-a913-7f347a456af6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343211} {:answer/id #uuid "8cd97f9c-844f-56b5-817d-e6e4e1cb664e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343470} :answer-field/id #uuid "de45b388-2b67-538a-b0bc-8012752c54d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344882}] :question/id #uuid "2ba66124-4bc1-5252-bf4a-2277af1637ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-75611" :question/problem "The eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 20-4k & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2k+1$ is  $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Determine the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 20-4k & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =(2k+1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 26-4k\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2k+1 \\\\ 4k+2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}7 &=2k+1, \\\\ 26-4k &=4k+2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n7=2k+1\\,⟹\\,k=3.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $k=3$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}26-4k & =4k+2 \\\\ 26-4\\cdot 3 & =4\\cdot 3+2 \\\\ 14 & =14,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369017 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0754ef11-4dc9-51ac-abc9-eb129fb02fde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369019} {:answer/id #uuid "99ba0c87-96ff-5bca-8ff5-fc7cb14f5450" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369020} {:answer/id #uuid "6c74d31a-8e96-5ed0-bad8-a9dcfc0ebead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369021} {:answer/id #uuid "9dc4a8af-a5a9-521b-9f5e-4b5a09f5a3aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369022} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ff71e1-6371-5ba8-8037-1b218de08f44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369020} :answer-field/id #uuid "9803491e-970a-5293-8490-ede16c766d95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369018}] :question/id #uuid "c8e7a988-77b8-5a0f-9bec-d0db9f889455" :question/math-academy-id "q-106896" :question/problem "The eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2k+1 & -k\\end{bmatrix}$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2k$ is  $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Determine the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2k+1 & -k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =2k\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ k+1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2k \\\\ 2k\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2 &=2k, \\\\ k+1 &=2k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2=2k\\,⟹\\,k=1.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $k=1$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k+1 & =2k \\\\ 1+1 & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\ 2 & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495152 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77a2513c-453d-5a29-8fe9-2a67c6dc9c03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494577} {:answer/id #uuid "8a34feff-d7af-5be0-bb1e-052359fe25b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494627} {:answer/id #uuid "9fbd3cfe-59fd-5bb3-8ca5-8b31a8b5e03c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494681} {:answer/id #uuid "a457dd93-1e88-5e06-a387-62d51340e2b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494692} {:answer/id #uuid "bee15731-1afa-57b9-a681-55ceed3bd405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494692} :answer-field/id #uuid "76b6e9d4-78c1-58c7-893c-a4f2cb9f0a8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494968}] :question/id #uuid "dbb0dde9-c40d-5e25-859f-e2de0b0b8937" :question/math-academy-id "q-75605" :question/problem "An eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$ \ncorresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda =3k-2$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 5k\\end{bmatrix}.$ Determine the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 5k\\end{bmatrix} & =(3k-2)\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 5k\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -30+35k\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6(3k-2) \\\\ (5k)(3k-2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -30+35k\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-18k+12 \\\\ 15{k}^{2}-10k\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6 &=-18k+12 \\\\ -30+35k &=15{k}^{2}-10k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-6=-18k+12\\,⟹\\,k=1.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $k=1$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-30+35k & =15{k}^{2}-10k \\\\ -30+35\\cdot 1 & =15\\cdot {1}^{2}-10\\cdot 1 \\\\ 5 & =5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=1.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Given an Eigenvalue and Eigenvector of a Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105787 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105628 :question/id #uuid "df40f37e-0d8f-5eb2-ad8a-9257440851df" :question/math-academy-id "e-5891" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fdfa0528b5476ed0246679c45fd31824470063a68f33c4c9af0f714ad201538f.png)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $2.$ Find the vector $A\\mathbf{v}.$" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvector ![](images/8d/8dd3f3b1b65f44a9d1db82d12e4dbf25e764d85c8bdfcd4538750a0e51c41d5d.svg) corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av=2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](images/78/78e1e3c015adad2c826a64e255bd982941dc9fe50679acc2bae3f00b31bd9f39.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49c501da-161c-53be-8349-571cc6a07975" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106295 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83504059-f96d-5f5a-bfd0-9c802e286d8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/33/33789f7649f286c604758ef2d9faa2c43c1bba934d1cf81d2dc7cd729451d51a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103919} {:answer/id #uuid "90de6065-341c-5fd7-ab51-2b16da339c72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2a/2a7b930cfa3f15341645ca9f54abf7c39de781619abf2fed540538b2d291b207.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103920} {:answer/id #uuid "9801809f-fa57-5ec3-9ed8-2f2f804cc1a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e9/e91b43bf7cbeec0d357cfbf3921ef47880fe4ebbf581edf3de8bad3083dede13.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103921} {:answer/id #uuid "2f40b09d-85ca-5cf6-968b-0c624dade63a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/88/88c71f260edf5083f275c8be7fde3915adcbda3cb5f8be49d90a911bbb056218.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103922} {:answer/id #uuid "6b84804c-9cda-54e6-80a6-6764d27889b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6f/6fd33e143a769555c5a5057a7ba5977a8f8dbaa5cae41c